A Boundary-Characterisation Programme for the Uniform Excess-Charge Theory of the Exchange-Free Engel–Dreizler Functional

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM

Abstract

This thesis addresses the precise boundary of the invariant coupling domain on which the exchange-free Engel–Dreizler functional admits a uniform molecular excess-charge bound. Benguria and Siedentop (arXiv:2611.14497, 2026) have proved that under the restrictions

\gamma:=\alpha\lambda\le\frac{1}{1233},\qquad \chi_k:=\alpha^3Z_k^2\le\frac{1}{137}\quad(1\le k\le K)

every finite-mass electronic minimiser with non-positive total energy satisfies

Q:=N-\sum_{k=1}^K Z_k<\frac{5913}{1000}\,\lambda^{3/2}K,

which at \lambda=1/9 gives Q<0.219\,K. The proof combines molecular Sommerfeld estimates with a convex nonlinear comparison in the variable F(p); the failure of this comparison at high density is represented by a non-negative Newton potential, and a quartic coercivity estimate obtained from nuclear repulsion and a signed localised Hardy inequality controls the mass of this source. The exterior estimates depend only on \gamma and are already free of the \chi_k-restriction. The restriction \chi_k\le1/137 enters exclusively through the coercivity step, which must absorb the high-density source mass \int S\le36\pi\alpha\,N_{\mathrm{high}}.

This thesis characterises the boundary at which that absorption fails. The central analytical vehicle is a scaling analysis of the radial atomic Euler–Poisson system near the nucleus. A dominant-balance argument, constrained by the mass bound N_{\mathrm{high}}\le N=Z_{\mathrm{tot}}+O(1), identifies the inner scaling p(r)\sim Z(\log Z)^{-1/2}v(Zr) up to logarithmic corrections. Within this admissible class, the question is whether N_{\mathrm{high}} remains uniformly bounded as \chi\to\infty at fixed \gamma. The thesis establishes, conditionally on an inner-scaling hypothesis that is supported by numerical continuation across Z\in[10^2,10^6], that any unbounded growth of N_{\mathrm{high}}, however slow, is incompatible with the BS26 absorption condition \int S\le c_0(\gamma)=O(1). The obstruction is therefore structural within the BS26 architecture, but it does not exclude a fundamentally different coercivity mechanism that avoids the source-mass bound entirely. The programme is atomic-first, source-mass-centric, and audit-driven: a rational-arithmetic audit of the BS26 proof classifies every occurrence of the two restrictions as tight, loose, or structural; a corrected radial solver supplies the diagnostic scaling laws; and a conditional molecular extension tests whether geometry uniformity survives the coalescence limit on the largest domain compatible with the atomic obstruction. A rigorous obstruction is treated as a co-equal primary outcome with a rigorous extension.

