Introduction

1. The Excess-Charge Problem

The excess-charge problem for density-functional models of electronic matter asks how many electrons a finite nuclear configuration can bind beyond neutrality. For the non-relativistic Thomas–Fermi–Dirac–von Weizsäcker (TFDW) functional, Frank, Nam, and Van den Bosch proved that a nucleus of charge Z>0 can bind at most Z+C electrons, where C is a universal constant. For two distinct nuclei of total charge Z, Chen and Wu proved

N\le Z+C Z^{153/154}(1+\log Z)^{3/8},

with constants independent of the nuclear positions and charges, using a comparison with the neutral Thomas–Fermi density and localisation and exterior L^1-estimates coupled through the screened potential. These results rest on the quadratic gradient term |\nabla\sqrt{\rho}|^2 of the von Weizsäcker correction, which penalises high-density concentration strongly enough to enforce near-neutrality uniformly in Z.

The relativistic exchange-free Engel–Dreizler functional replaces the quadratic gradient by a term derived from quantum electrodynamics. Its kinetic structure is encoded in the function

f(p)^2=\frac{p}{\sqrt{1+p^2}}+\frac{2p^2}{1+p^2}\operatorname{arsinh}p,

where p is the Fermi momentum. At large p,

f(p)^2\sim2\log(2p),\qquad F(p):=pf(p)\sim p\sqrt{2\log(2p)},\qquad j(p):=\frac{p^2}{f(p)}\sim\frac{p^2}{\sqrt{2\log(2p)}}.

The gradient |\nabla F(p)|^2 grows only logarithmically. The renormalisation in Engel’s and Dreizler’s derivation, which produces the \operatorname{arsinh} function, is precisely what ensures the lower boundedness of the functional for all nuclear charges. But logarithmic boundedness is not the same as uniform excess-charge control. The question that this thesis addresses is whether the logarithmic softness of the Engel–Dreizler gradient permits the high-density core mass to grow without bound as the nuclear charge increases, and if so, at what rate and with what consequence for the excess-charge bound.

2. The Benguria–Siedentop Theorem and Its Architectural Boundaries

Benguria and Siedentop (BS26) have proved the first uniform molecular excess-charge bound for the Engel–Dreizler functional. Under the invariant coupling restrictions

\alpha\lambda\le\frac{1}{1233},\qquad \alpha^3Z_k^2\le\frac{1}{137}\quad(1\le k\le K),

every finite-mass electronic minimiser whose total energy, including nuclear repulsion, is non-positive satisfies

N-\sum_{k=1}^K Z_k<\frac{5913}{1000}\,\lambda^{3/2}K.

At \lambda=1/9, this gives Q<0.219\,K. The proof uses no symmetry of the density, no restriction on the total nuclear charge, and no minimum nuclear separation. The energy hypothesis holds automatically for atoms and for local minima of the Born–Oppenheimer energy at which the electronic infimum is attained.

The proof architecture has three layers. The first is a set of molecular Sommerfeld estimates that depend only on \gamma=\alpha\lambda. These exterior estimates are already charge- and geometry-independent. The second is a convex nonlinear comparison in the variable F(p); its failure at very high density is represented by a non-negative Newton potential S. The third is a quartic coercivity estimate, obtained from nuclear repulsion and a signed localised Hardy inequality, which controls the total mass of S. All finite numerical inequalities are reduced to rational arithmetic.

The restriction \chi_k\le1/137 enters exclusively through the third layer. The coercivity estimate must absorb the source mass \int S, and the only bound available for this quantity is

\int_{\mathbb{R}^3}S\le36\pi\alpha\,N_{\mathrm{high}},\qquad N_{\mathrm{high}}:=\int_{\{p>p_{\mathrm{cut}}\}}\rho.

The cutoff is exponentially large, p_{\mathrm{cut}}=\exp(180/(1233\gamma)), and the absorption step requires N_{\mathrm{high}} to be small enough that \int S can be controlled by the positive terms in the quartic form. The restriction \chi_k\le1/137 is the condition under which this absorption succeeds with the rational constants used in the proof.

The boundary problem is therefore the following. The exterior layer is free of the \chi_k-restriction. The restriction enters through the interaction between the high-density failure of the convex comparison and the coercivity estimate that must absorb its source. If N_{\mathrm{high}} remains uniformly bounded as \chi\to\infty at fixed \gamma, the restriction is an artifact of the particular rational inequalities used in the coercivity step, and a better estimate would remove it. If N_{\mathrm{high}} grows without bound, the restriction is structural within the BS26 architecture: no improvement of the constants can compensate for a source mass that diverges.

3. The Logarithmic Softness and the Admissible Scaling Class

The central structural feature of the Engel–Dreizler functional is the logarithmic growth of the kinetic gradient. In TFDW theory, the quadratic gradient |\nabla\sqrt{\rho}|^2 provides a penalty against concentration that scales as a positive power of the density, and this penalty is what enforces N\le Z+C uniformly. In the Engel–Dreizler model, the penalty scales only as \log p. The question is whether this logarithmic penalty is sufficient to keep the high-density mass bounded, or whether it is too weak.

The admissible scaling class is constrained by the mass bound. For any minimiser satisfying the BS26 bound,

N=Z_{\mathrm{tot}}+Q,\qquad Q<C_0(\gamma)K,\qquad C_0(\gamma)=\frac{5913}{1000}\lambda^{3/2}.

Hence N_{\mathrm{high}}\le N=Z_{\mathrm{tot}}+O(1) at fixed \gamma. Any scaling ansatz that produces N_{\mathrm{high}}\gg Z_{\mathrm{tot}} is incompatible with the existence of a finite-mass minimiser. In particular, if p(0)\sim Z^\beta and the core radius satisfies r_c\sim Z^{-1}, then N_{\mathrm{high}}\sim Z^{3\beta-3}, and the mass constraint requires \beta\le4/3. A dominant-balance analysis of the radial Euler–Poisson system in the regime where the kinetic term Z^\beta v dominates the Coulomb term Z^2/\xi gives 2+\beta=3\beta, hence \beta=1, up to logarithmic corrections. There is no dominant balance that produces \beta=3/2. The exponent 3/2 is the critical exponent for power-law kinetic terms p^\alpha in generalised Thomas–Fermi–Weizsäcker functionals, where the behaviour changes from a uniform bound to a linear bound at the critical coupling 4\sqrt{\pi}. It does not transfer to the \operatorname{arsinh}-type Engel–Dreizler functional.

The correct scaling is therefore

p(0)\sim Z(\log Z)^{-1/2},\qquad r_c\sim Z^{-1},

with a core mass that is at most O(1) up to logarithmic factors. The question of whether this core mass is uniformly bounded or grows without bound—even logarithmically—is the precise boundary question of the thesis.

4. Research Questions

The thesis is organised around four questions.

RQ1 (Audit). At which precise points in the BS26 proof do the restrictions \gamma\le1/1233 and \chi_k\le1/137 enter? Which are tight, which are loose, and which are structural?

RQ2 (Scaling). For the radial atomic problem, what is the correct inner-core scaling of the Fermi momentum p(r) as Z\to\infty at fixed \gamma? Does the logarithmic softness of |\nabla F(p)|^2 permit N_{\mathrm{high}} to remain uniformly bounded, or does it force unbounded growth?

RQ3 (Source mass). Can the high-density source mass \int S be bounded independently of p_{\mathrm{cut}} by a device that does not pass through the BS26 absorption mechanism?

RQ4 (Molecular uniformity). If an atomic obstruction is established, can a conditional molecular extension survive the coalescence limit R_k\to R_l on the largest domain compatible with the obstruction?

A negative answer to RQ2 or RQ3 is a rigorous obstruction; a negative answer to RQ4 is a sharp statement about the limits of geometry uniformity. All are legitimate outcomes under the categorisation protocol of Section 7.

5. The Obstruction and Its Logical Status

The central claim of the thesis is conditional and must be stated with precision.

Assumption (Inner scaling). There exists \gamma_0>0 such that for all \gamma\in(0,\gamma_0], the radial atomic minimiser satisfies p(0)\sim Z(\log Z)^{-1/2} up to bounded factors, with the saturation radius r_{\mathrm{sat}}\sim Z/p(0).

Proposition (Conditional obstruction). Assume the inner-scaling hypothesis. If N_{\mathrm{high}} is not uniformly bounded as \chi\to\infty at fixed \gamma, then there exists \chi_{\mathrm{crit}}(\gamma)<\infty such that for all \chi>\chi_{\mathrm{crit}} the BS26 absorption condition

\int S\le c_0(\gamma)

fails. Equivalently, at fixed \gamma, the BS26 architecture cannot extend the excess-charge bound uniformly to \chi\to\infty.

The proposition is Conditional on the inner-scaling hypothesis and on the unboundedness of N_{\mathrm{high}}. It is not a proof that the Engel–Dreizler functional itself admits unbounded excess charge; it is a proof that any mechanism relying on the source-mass bound \int S\le36\pi\alpha N_{\mathrm{high}} and feeding it into the quartic coercivity estimate cannot handle \chi\to\infty uniformly.

The thesis is structured so that this conditional obstruction is a first-class outcome. If the inner-scaling hypothesis is established rigorously, the obstruction is upgraded to Proved within the BS26 architecture. If an alternative coercivity mechanism is found that avoids the source-mass bound, the obstruction is superseded and Outcome A (unconditional removal of \chi_k) becomes available. Both branches are analytically substantive.

6. Methodology

The programme proceeds in four stages.

Stage 1: Rational-arithmetic audit. The BS26 proof is reconstructed in exact rational arithmetic. Every occurrence of \gamma\le1/1233 and \chi_k\le1/137 is isolated and classified as tight, loose, or structural. The deliverable is an entry-point map accompanied by a reproducibility notebook recording the slack at each finite inequality.

Stage 2: Atomic scaling analysis. The radial Euler–Poisson system is analysed with the scaling ansatz \xi=Zr, p(r)=Z^\beta v(\xi). The mass constraint N_{\mathrm{high}}\le Z_{\mathrm{tot}}+O(1) is imposed as a filter on admissible \beta. Three analytical routes are pursued: a direct scaling inequality using the logarithmic form of the kinetic gradient; a model comparison in which f(p)^2 is replaced by 2\log(2p) in the high-density region, with the physical saturation cut-off r_{\mathrm{sat}}\sim Z/p(0) imposed; and a coercivity-gap analysis exhibiting trial configurations on which the quartic form becomes negative as \chi\to\infty. The numerical continuation across Z\in[10^2,10^6] supplies diagnostic scaling laws for p(0), r_c, N, Q, N_{>p_0^{\mathrm{diag}}}, and the proxy p(0)^3r_c^3, subject to the mass-consistency filter.

Stage 3: Conditional molecular extension. If Stage 2 yields a rigorous obstruction, the molecular stage characterises the largest domain on which a conditional extension survives. Two directions are pursued: a screened local Hardy analysis, in which the effective charge seen by electrons is reduced by the electron cloud, potentially allowing the local inequality to hold for a larger bare Z_k; and a union-type local mass bound, in which the high-density set is contained in a union of balls centred at the nuclei, with mass in each ball bounded uniformly and the bound surviving the coalescence limit R_k\to R_l. The Chen–Wu architecture for two-centre TFDW supplies a template, though no estimate transfers verbatim because the Engel–Dreizler kinetic term is \operatorname{arsinh}-type rather than power-law.

Stage 4: Synthesis. The results of Stages 1–3 are combined into a boundary-characterisation theorem with the following structure: a precise statement of the obstruction, including the inequality at which the BS26 mechanism fails and the admissible growth rates of N_{\mathrm{high}}; a precise statement of any conditional extension and the additional hypotheses required; sharpness examples or trial configurations that saturate the obstruction; a comparison with the BS26 constants identifying which are tight and which are loose; and a discussion of what a fundamentally different coercivity mechanism would need to accomplish in order to evade the obstruction.

7. Categorisation Protocol

Every claim in the thesis is tagged as one of four types. Proved claims are established either in BS26 or in the present work with a complete proof. Conditional claims are established only under explicitly stated hypotheses not known to hold in general. Conjectured claims are proposed without proof, with motivation. Numerical claims are supported by computation with reported precision, mesh convergence, and residuals. No numerical observation is presented as an asymptotic theorem. No failure of a specific mechanism is presented as a disproof of the desired bound unless a rigorous obstruction is supplied.

8. Expected Outcomes and Their Status

The thesis has four possible outcomes, ordered by strength.

Outcome A (unconditional removal of \chi_k). A proof that Q\le C(\gamma)K for all \chi_k>0 at fixed \gamma. The logarithmic softness of the kinetic gradient makes this outcome unlikely within the BS26 mechanism, but it is not excluded a priori; the coercivity-gap analysis of Stage 2 is designed to test whether an alternative device can evade the source-mass obstruction.

Outcome B (conditional extension). A proof that Q\le C(\gamma,\chi)K holds on a domain strictly larger than the BS26 domain, with an explicit trade-off between \gamma and \chi, combined with a sharpness example showing that the trade-off cannot be improved within the current mechanism.

Outcome C (structural obstruction). A rigorous proof that N_{\mathrm{high}} is not uniformly bounded as \chi\to\infty at fixed \gamma, within any mechanism that controls the source mass through \int S\le36\pi\alpha N_{\mathrm{high}}. The obstruction is tagged Conditional if the growth is established under the inner-scaling hypothesis and Proved if it is derived from the Euler–Poisson system without additional hypotheses. Outcome C is treated as a co-equal primary objective with Outcomes A and B.

Outcome D (supporting). The rational-arithmetic audit, the corrected radial solver, and the diagnostic scaling laws. These are publishable independently as a reproducibility and benchmarking contribution.

The thesis is structured so that Outcome C or Outcome B, supported by Outcome D, constitutes a complete and defensible contribution if Outcome A is not achieved.

9. Feasibility and Decision Structure

The hardest analytic infrastructure is already supplied by BS26: the exterior comparison, the nonlinear comparison, the source-mass representation, the coercivity architecture, and the absorption. The programme inherits these and asks only where the restrictions can be relaxed, or why they cannot. The audit is bounded and finite, and it is directly supported by BS26’s reduction of all finite inequalities to rational arithmetic. The radial system is well-posed and amenable to Chebyshev collocation with logarithmic grading. The atomic-first strategy is forced by the proof structure: BS26 uses nuclear repulsion in the molecular coercivity step and a different global Hardy device in the atomic step, so any new mechanism must be tested atomically first.

A decision point is fixed at the midpoint of Stage 2. If the scaling analysis has not produced a rigorous obstruction or a rigorous extension by that point, the programme pivots to Outcome C in its Conditional form, supported by the Stage 1 audit and the numerical diagnostics, and Stage 3 is restricted to the screened-Hardy and union-bound directions on the conditional domain. The audit and the numerical diagnostics are secured before the decision point, so the thesis core is defensible regardless of the analytic outcome.

1. The Established Theory and the Locus of the Boundary Problem

Benguria and Siedentop (arXiv:2611.14497, 10 November 2026; hereafter BS26) prove an explicit molecular excess-charge bound for the exchange-free Engel–Dreizler functional. Under the invariant coupling restrictions

\gamma:=\alpha\lambda\le\frac{1}{1233},\qquad \chi_k:=\alpha^3Z_k^2\le\frac{1}{137}\quad(1\le k\le K), \tag{1.1}

every finite-mass electronic minimiser whose total energy, including nuclear repulsion, is non-positive satisfies

Q:=N-\sum_{k=1}^K Z_k<\frac{5913}{1000}\,\lambda^{3/2}K. \tag{1.2}

At the physical value \lambda=1/9, this yields Q<0.219\,K. The proof uses no symmetry of the density, no restriction on the total nuclear charge, and no minimum nuclear separation. The energy hypothesis holds automatically for atoms and for local minima of the Born–Oppenheimer energy at which the electronic infimum is attained.

Three architectural features of BS26 are directly relevant to any boundary-characterisation programme.

(A) Exterior estimates depend only on \gamma. BS26 combine “molecular Sommerfeld estimates” with a convex nonlinear comparison in the variable F(p). The exterior layer is therefore already uniform in the variables that the \chi_k-restriction constrains.

(B) The convex comparison fails at high density, and the failure is a non-negative Newton potential. The comparison in F(p) breaks down at very high density, and this failure “is represented by a nonnegative Newton potential”. The source mass of this Newton potential is what the coercivity step must absorb.

(C) The coercivity step controls the source mass. A “quartic coercivity estimate, obtained from nuclear repulsion and a signed localized Hardy inequality, controls the total mass of this source”. In the molecular case, nuclear repulsion supplies the necessary positive term; in the atomic case, a different global Hardy device must be used. The restriction \chi_k\le1/137 enters precisely at this step.

The rational-arithmetic reduction of all finite numerical inequalities is a further structural feature of BS26. It makes an independent audit both feasible and directly informative.

The boundary problem is therefore the following. The exterior layer (A) is already free of the \chi_k-restriction. The restriction enters exclusively through the interaction between (B) and (C): the coercivity estimate must absorb a source mass that is generated by the high-density failure of the convex comparison. If that source mass grows without bound as \chi\to\infty at fixed \gamma, then no improvement of the constants in the coercivity estimate can compensate, and the \chi_k-restriction is structural within the BS26 architecture. If the source mass remains bounded, the restriction is an artifact of the particular rational inequalities used in (C). The programme is designed to decide between these alternatives.

A necessary constraint on any such analysis is the mass bound. For a minimiser satisfying (1.2), N=Z_{\mathrm{tot}}+Q with Q=O(1). Hence

N_{\mathrm{high}}:=\int_{\{p>p_{\mathrm{cut}}\}}\rho\le N=Z_{\mathrm{tot}}+O(1). \tag{1.3}

Any scaling ansatz for N_{\mathrm{high}} that grows faster than Z_{\mathrm{tot}} is therefore incompatible with the existence of a finite-mass minimiser. This constraint is used below as a filter on admissible scaling regimes.

