2. The Two Invariant Parameters and the Precise Question

The two invariant parameters of the problem are

\gamma=\alpha\lambda,\qquad \chi_k=\alpha^3Z_k^2=\gamma\kappa_k^2,\quad \kappa_k=\frac{\alpha Z_k}{\sqrt{\lambda}}. \tag{2.1}

Every statement about extending the domain must specify which parameter varies, along which path, and at what cost in the constant of (1.2).

The restriction \gamma\le1/1233 enters through the exterior Sommerfeld estimates and through the numerical smallness required for the final absorption step. The restriction \chi_k\le1/137 enters through the quartic coercivity estimate. The audit of Section 3 classifies each occurrence as tight (saturable by the proof’s own inequalities), loose (improvable by better rational constants), or structural (forced by the mechanism).

The central analytical question is whether the source mass \int S can be bounded uniformly as \chi\to\infty at fixed \gamma. The BS26 bound is

\int_{\mathbb{R}^3}S\le36\pi\alpha\,N_{\mathrm{high}}. \tag{2.2}

If N_{\mathrm{high}} is uniformly bounded in \chi at fixed \gamma, the coercivity estimate can absorb the source for all sufficiently large Z_k, and the \chi_k-restriction is removable. If N_{\mathrm{high}} grows without bound, the absorption fails at some finite \chi_{\mathrm{crit}}, and the restriction is structural within the BS26 mechanism.

A result of Benguria and Siedentop for generalised Thomas–Fermi–Weizsäcker functionals provides an instructive contrast. For power-law kinetic terms p^\alpha, the case \alpha=3/2 is critical: for 3/2<\alpha<2 the excess charge is uniformly bounded in Z, while at \alpha=3/2 the behaviour changes from a uniform bound to a linear bound at the critical coupling 4\sqrt{\pi} of the nonlinear term. The Engel–Dreizler kinetic term grows as p^2/\sqrt{\log p}, which is softer than any power p^\alpha with \alpha>1. The existence of a critical power in the generalised family motivates the search for an analogous critical behaviour in the logarithmically corrected Engel–Dreizler model, but the critical exponent 3/2 of the power-law family does not transfer to the Engel–Dreizler setting; the scaling balance of the latter must be derived independently.

