4. Stage 2: Atomic Scaling Analysis and the Structural Question

4.1 The radial Euler–Poisson system

For a radial atomic minimiser with one nucleus at the origin, away from r=0, the Euler–Poisson system is

-\frac{3\lambda}{4}\Big(P''+\frac{2}{r}P'\Big)=j(p)\big(T(p)-t\big), \tag{4.1}

t''+\frac{2}{r}t'=4\pi\alpha\,\rho,\qquad \rho=\frac{p^3}{3\pi^2}, \tag{4.2}

with t=\alpha\varphi-\eta, \eta\ge0, boundary conditions t(r)\sim+\alpha Z/r as r\downarrow0 and t(r)\to-\eta as r\to\infty, and P=F(p), P'(0)=0, P\to0 as r\to\infty.

The Engel–Dreizler functions satisfy, at large p,

f(p)^2\sim2\log(2p),\quad F(p)\sim p\sqrt{2\log(2p)},\quad j(p)\sim\frac{p^2}{\sqrt{2\log(2p)}},\quad T(p)\sim p. \tag{4.3}

The kinetic gradient therefore grows only logarithmically: |\nabla F(p)|^2\sim2\log(2p)|\nabla p|^2. This is the structural feature that distinguishes Engel–Dreizler from Thomas–Fermi–von Weizsäcker, where the gradient term grows quadratically and provides a strong penalty against concentration. In the Thomas–Fermi–Dirac–von Weizsäcker theory, the quadratic gradient term yields the bound N\le Z+C for a single nucleus (Frank–Nam–Van den Bosch, Comm. Pure Appl. Math. 71 (2018), 577–614), and for two distinct nuclei the bound N\le Z+CZ^{153/154}(1+\log Z)^{3/8}, uniform in nuclear positions and charges. The Engel–Dreizler model does not inherit these quadratic-gradient penalties.

4.2 The scaling balance and the role of logarithmic softness

Introduce the inner variable and scaling ansatz

\xi=Zr,\qquad p(r)=Z^\beta v(\xi). \tag{4.4}

The Coulomb potential near the nucleus is t(r)\sim\alpha Z/r=\alpha Z^2/\xi. The kinetic term scales as

\Delta_r F(p)\sim Z^{2+\beta}\sqrt{\log Z}\,\Delta_\xi v, \tag{4.5}

while the right-hand side of (4.1) scales as

j(p)\big(T(p)-t\big)\sim\frac{Z^{2\beta}}{\sqrt{\log Z}}\Big(Z^\beta v-\frac{\alpha Z^2}{\xi}\Big). \tag{4.6}

Balancing (4.5) and (4.6) in the regime \beta>1, where the kinetic term Z^\beta v dominates the Coulomb term Z^2/\xi in the parenthesis, gives 2+\beta=3\beta, hence \beta=1. In the regime \beta<1, the Coulomb term dominates and the balance gives \beta=0. There is no dominant balance that produces a power \beta>1 for the Engel–Dreizler kinetic term. The exponent \beta=1, up to logarithmic corrections, is the natural scaling of the inner core.

The numerical solution of the radial system across Z\in[10^2,10^6], reported in Section 5, is consistent with a central momentum growing no faster than Z(\log Z)^{-1/2} and a core radius shrinking as Z^{-1} up to logarithmic factors. The precise effective exponent over the accessible range is a Numerical observation, not an asymptotic theorem.

4.3 The source-mass question and the admissible obstruction

The mass constraint (1.3) excludes any scaling ansatz for N_{\mathrm{high}} that grows faster than Z_{\mathrm{tot}}. The question of whether the \chi_k-restriction is structural must therefore be posed in terms of growth rates compatible with N_{\mathrm{high}}\le Z_{\mathrm{tot}}+O(1).

The BS26 absorption step requires

\int S\le c_0(\gamma) \tag{4.7}

for some finite c_0(\gamma). Because \int S\le36\pi\alpha N_{\mathrm{high}}, any unbounded growth of N_{\mathrm{high}} at fixed \gamma, however slow, is incompatible with (4.7) for sufficiently large \chi. The admissible obstruction is therefore not a polynomial growth claim such as N_{\mathrm{high}}\sim Z^{3/2}, which would violate (1.3), but the weaker and more robust statement that N_{\mathrm{high}} is not uniformly bounded. Logarithmic growth, or growth as a small power Z^{\epsilon} with \epsilon constrained by (1.3), suffices.

Three analytical routes are pursued to establish or exclude such growth.

Route 1: Direct scaling inequality. Prove that the energy of the inner core is bounded below by a term that grows without bound as N_{\mathrm{high}} increases, using the logarithmic form of the kinetic gradient. The logarithmic softness of |\nabla F(p)|^2 is a plausible mechanism for unbounded growth, but the rate must be derived, not assumed.

Route 2: Model comparison. Replace f(p)^2 by 2\log(2p) in the high-density region and analyse the resulting model Euler–Poisson system with the physical saturation at r_{\mathrm{sat}}\sim Z/p(0) imposed. The model must be solved with the mass constraint (1.3) enforced; without this constraint, the model integral diverges at the origin in a way that is cut off only by the finite central momentum. The model can establish the mechanism of slow growth, but not a polynomial rate.

Route 3: Coercivity-gap analysis. Exhibit a sequence of trial configurations on which the quartic coercivity form of BS26 becomes negative as \chi\to\infty at fixed \gamma, thereby showing that the coercivity estimate cannot hold beyond a finite \chi regardless of the constants. This is the most direct route to a structural obstruction, because it targets the precise inequality at which the \chi_k-restriction enters.

The Stage 2 deliverable is either (a) a rigorous atomic extension on a domain strictly larger than (1.1), or (b) a rigorous obstruction showing that N_{\mathrm{high}} is not uniformly bounded at fixed \gamma, with the growth mechanism identified. A rigorous proof that N_{\mathrm{high}} remains bounded would be a positive resolution of Outcome A; a rigorous proof of unbounded growth would be the structural obstruction of Outcome C. Both are first-class outcomes.

