5. Numerical Diagnostics: Correct Status and Interpretation

The radial system (4.1)–(4.2) is solved on a graded logarithmic mesh resolving inner scales down to O(Z^{-1}), with Chebyshev collocation and Newton–Krylov continuation in Z. The diagnostics are p(0), the core radius r_c defined by p(r_c)=p(0)/2 (not p(r_c)=1; the two definitions differ by a Z-dependent factor and must not be conflated), the total mass N, the excess charge Q, the diagnostic core mass N_{>p_0^{\mathrm{diag}}} at moderate thresholds p_0^{\mathrm{diag}}\in\{1,10,10^2,10^3\}, and the product p(0)^3r_c^3 as a proxy for the size of the high-density region.

The mass constraint (1.3) is imposed as a consistency check: any computed configuration for which the proxy p(0)^3r_c^3 exceeds N=Z_{\mathrm{tot}}+Q indicates that r_c is not the radius on which the density is approximately p(0)/2, and the proxy is discarded.

The numerical outputs are classified as Numerical under the categorisation protocol of Section 7. A fitted exponent over a finite Z-range is evidence, not an asymptotic theorem. The diagnostic core masses at moderate thresholds are the relevant quantities for testing whether the source mass shows any tendency to saturate; the true high-density region \{p>p_{\mathrm{cut}}\} is empty at physical parameters for computationally accessible Z, so the source mass must be inferred from the trend of the diagnostic masses rather than measured directly.

The numerical work supports the analytical routes of Section 4.3 by supplying scaling information and by testing candidate trial configurations for Route 3. It does not, by itself, establish or refute the structural obstruction.

