7. Categorisation Protocol

Every claim is tagged as one of:

· Proved: established in BS26 or in the present work, with a complete proof.
· Conditional: established only under explicitly stated hypotheses not known to hold in general.
· Conjectured: proposed without proof, with motivation.
· Numerical: supported by computation with reported precision, mesh convergence, and residuals.

No numerical observation is presented as an asymptotic theorem. No failure of a specific mechanism is presented as a disproof of the desired bound unless a rigorous obstruction is supplied.

---

8. Expected Outcomes

Outcome C (structural obstruction). A rigorous proof that N_{\mathrm{high}} is not uniformly bounded as \chi\to\infty at fixed \gamma, within any mechanism that controls the source mass through the bound (2.2). The obstruction is tagged Conditional if the growth of N_{\mathrm{high}} is established under an explicitly stated inner-scaling hypothesis, and Proved if the growth is derived from the Euler–Poisson system without additional hypotheses. The obstruction identifies the precise inequality at which the BS26 mechanism fails and quantifies the admissible growth rate. It is a co-equal primary objective with an extension.

Outcome B (conditional extension). A proof that Q\le C(\gamma,\chi)K holds on a domain strictly larger than (1.1), with an explicit trade-off between \gamma and \chi, combined with a sharpness example showing that the trade-off cannot be improved within the current mechanism.

Outcome A (unconditional removal of \chi_k). A proof that Q\le C(\gamma)K for all \chi_k>0 at fixed \gamma. The structural softness of the logarithmic gradient makes this outcome unlikely within the BS26 mechanism, but it is not excluded a priori; Route 3 of Stage 2 is designed to test whether an alternative coercivity device can evade the source-mass obstruction.

Outcome D (supporting). The Stage 1 rational-arithmetic audit, the corrected radial solver, and the diagnostic scaling laws. These are publishable independently as a reproducibility and benchmarking contribution.

The thesis is structured so that Outcome C or Outcome B, supported by Outcome D, constitutes a complete and defensible contribution if Outcome A is not achieved.

---

9. Feasibility and Decision Structure

The hardest analytic infrastructure is already supplied by BS26: the exterior comparison, the nonlinear comparison, the source-mass representation, the coercivity architecture, and the absorption. The programme inherits these and asks only where the restrictions can be relaxed, or why they cannot.

The audit is bounded and finite, and it is directly supported by BS26’s reduction of all finite inequalities to rational arithmetic. The radial system is well-posed and amenable to Chebyshev collocation with logarithmic grading. The atomic-first strategy is forced by the proof structure: BS26 uses nuclear repulsion in the molecular coercivity step and a different global Hardy device in the atomic step, so any new mechanism must be tested atomically first.

A decision point is fixed at the midpoint of Stage 2. If the scaling analysis has not produced a rigorous obstruction or a rigorous extension by that point, the programme pivots to Outcome C in its Conditional form, supported by the Stage 1 audit and the numerical diagnostics, and Stage 3 is restricted to the screened-Hardy and union-bound directions on the conditional domain. The audit and the numerical diagnostics are secured before the decision point, so the thesis core is defensible regardless of the analytic outcome.

---

10. Summary

BS26 has settled the uniform excess-charge theorem inside the domain (1.1). The boundary problem is whether the \chi_k-restriction is structural or an artifact of the rational inequalities used in the coercivity step. The exterior layer is already free of the restriction; the question is whether the high-density source mass that the coercivity step must absorb can be uniformly bounded as \chi\to\infty at fixed \gamma.

The Engel–Dreizler kinetic gradient grows only logarithmically, in contrast to the quadratic gradient of Thomas–Fermi–Dirac–von Weizsäcker, where uniform bounds N\le Z+C and molecular bounds uniform in nuclear positions are available. This logarithmic softness is a plausible mechanism for unbounded growth of the source mass, but the admissible growth rates are constrained by the mass bound N_{\mathrm{high}}\le Z_{\mathrm{tot}}+O(1). The programme therefore targets a boundary-characterisation theorem in which the obstruction is a slow, possibly logarithmic, unboundedness of N_{\mathrm{high}}, rigorously derived or established under an explicit inner-scaling hypothesis, and in which any extension is conditional on evading this obstruction. The programme is atomic-first, source-mass-centric, and audit-driven, and it is structured so that a rigorous negative result is as valuable as a rigorous positive one.

