Appendix D. Formal Proof Sketch of the Conditional Obstruction

D.1 The mass constraint as a filter. For any minimiser satisfying (A.1),

N=Z_{\mathrm{tot}}+Q,\qquad Q<C_0(\gamma)K, \tag{D.1}

with C_0(\gamma)=(5913/1000)\lambda^{3/2}. Hence N=Z_{\mathrm{tot}}+O(1) at fixed \gamma. Since N_{\mathrm{high}}\le N, any scaling ansatz that produces N_{\mathrm{high}}\gg Z_{\mathrm{tot}} is incompatible with (D.1). In particular, an ansatz p(0)\sim Z^\beta with \beta>1 and r_c\sim Z^{-1} would give N_{\mathrm{high}}\sim Z^{3\beta-3}, which for \beta>4/3 violates N_{\mathrm{high}}\le Z_{\mathrm{tot}}+O(1). The admissible range for the effective exponent is therefore \beta\le4/3, and the dominant-balance analysis of Section 4.2 gives \beta=1 up to logarithmic corrections.

D.2 Inner scaling lemma (conditional). Let p be a regular radial solution of (4.1)–(4.2) with t(r)=\alpha Z/r+O(1) near the origin, satisfying the mass constraint (D.1). Suppose there exist Z_0, \beta\in(0,4/3], and c>0 such that p(0)\ge cZ^\beta for all Z\ge Z_0. Then there exist constants C_1,C_2>0, depending on \gamma but not on Z, such that for Z\ge Z_0,

N_{>p_0}(Z)\ge C_1 Z^{\max\{0,\,3\beta-3\}}(\log Z)^{-\beta/2} \tag{D.2}

for all p_0\le p(0)/2.

Proof sketch. By monotonicity of p on [0,r_c] (valid for radial minimisers), p(r)\ge p(0)/2 for r\le r_c:=p^{-1}(p(0)/2). Hence

N_{>p(0)/2}\ge\frac{4\pi}{3\pi^2}\Big(\frac{p(0)}{2}\Big)^3\frac{r_c^3}{3}. \tag{D.3}

The Coulomb lower bound on t together with the balance in (4.1) gives r_c\ge c'Z^{-1}. Substituting p(0)\ge cZ^\beta yields the power Z^{3\beta-3}. The logarithmic factor comes from the 1/\sqrt{\log p} correction in j(p) in (4.3); it does not change the power but contributes (\log Z)^{-\beta/2}. The maximum with 0 enforces the mass constraint: if 3\beta-3<0, the lower bound is O(1) and the mass constraint is not violated. \square

For the dominant-balance value \beta=1, Lemma D.2 gives N_{>p_0}\gtrsim(\log Z)^{-1/2}, which is bounded below but does not by itself prove divergence. The obstruction requires a lower bound that grows without bound, and the logarithmic softness of the kinetic gradient is the mechanism that must supply it.

D.3 Model Euler–Poisson system and the saturation cut-off. In the region \{p>p_*\gg1\}, replace f(p)^2 by 2\log(2p) and introduce u:=p\sqrt{2\log(2p)}, the leading term of F(p). The model system is

-\frac{3\lambda}{4}\Delta u=\frac{p^2}{\sqrt{2\log(2p)}}\big(p-t\big),\qquad \Delta t=4\pi\alpha\frac{p^3}{3\pi^2}. \tag{D.4}

Set \xi=Zr and p(r)=Z^\beta v(\xi). At \beta=1, the leading balance gives

\xi^2v''+2\xi v'=\mu(\xi)\,\frac{v^2}{\sqrt{\log Z}}\Big(v-\frac{\alpha}{\xi}\Big), \tag{D.5}

where \mu(\xi)>0 is slowly varying. The Coulomb singularity forces v(\xi)\sim\alpha/\xi as \xi\to0, but the physical solution saturates at the radius where p(r) reaches its finite central value p(0). The saturation radius is

r_{\mathrm{sat}}\sim\frac{Z}{p(0)}, \tag{D.6}

not r\sim Z^{-1}. The core mass integral

N_{\mathrm{high}}\sim\int_{r_{\mathrm{sat}}}^{Z^{-1}}\Big(\frac{Z}{r}\Big)^3r^2\,dr\sim Z^3\log\frac{Z^{-1}}{r_{\mathrm{sat}}}\sim Z^3\log\frac{p(0)}{Z^2} \tag{D.7}

is cut off at the lower limit r_{\mathrm{sat}}, not at the upper limit Z^{-1}. The integral therefore does not diverge; its value is controlled by the ratio p(0)/Z^2. If p(0)\sim Z(\log Z)^{-1/2} (the dominant-balance value up to logarithmic correction), then \log(p(0)/Z^2)\sim-\frac12\log\log Z, which is negative for large Z. The model therefore does not support a positive power-law growth of N_{\mathrm{high}}; the correct conclusion is that the model is consistent with a slowly varying, possibly logarithmically growing, core mass, and that the polynomial scaling Z^{3/2} is an artifact of an incorrect treatment of the saturation cut-off.

D.4 Precise statement of the conditional obstruction. Fix \gamma\in(0,1/1233]. Assume:

1. p_{\mathrm{cut}}(\gamma)=\exp(180/(1233\gamma)) as in BS26;
2. the absorption step requires \int S\le c_0, where c_0=c_0(\gamma) is the maximal source mass allowed by the coercivity estimate;
3. N_{\mathrm{high}} is not uniformly bounded as \chi\to\infty at fixed \gamma, i.e. there exists a sequence \chi_n\to\infty and minimisers with N_{\mathrm{high}}(\chi_n)\to\infty.

Then there exists \chi_{\mathrm{crit}}=\chi_{\mathrm{crit}}(\gamma,c_0)<\infty such that for \chi>\chi_{\mathrm{crit}}, no minimiser satisfies the BS26 absorption mechanism. Equivalently, at fixed \gamma, the BS26 architecture cannot extend the excess-charge bound (A.1) uniformly to \chi\to\infty.

Argument. By (2.2), \int S\le36\pi\alpha N_{\mathrm{high}}. Assumption 3 gives \int S\to\infty along the sequence. The absorption condition \int S\le c_0 fails for all sufficiently large n. Set \chi_{\mathrm{crit}}=\inf\{\chi:\int S(\chi)>c_0\}. Since \chi\propto Z^2, the critical value is finite whenever N_{\mathrm{high}} is unbounded. \square

The obstruction is Conditional on assumption 3. Establishing assumption 3 rigorously—by Route 1 (direct scaling inequality), Route 2 (model comparison with the correct saturation cut-off), or Route 3 (coercivity-gap analysis)—is the central analytical task of Stage 2. The obstruction does not require a polynomial growth rate; any unbounded growth, however slow, suffices, because the absorption condition is O(1).

D.5 Necessary versus sufficient conditions. The obstruction of D.4 is an obstruction within the BS26 absorption mechanism, not of the Engel–Dreizler functional itself. It proves that any argument relying on the source-mass bound (2.2) and feeding it into the quartic coercivity estimate cannot handle \chi\to\infty uniformly at fixed \gamma. It does not exclude a fundamentally different coercivity mechanism that avoids (2.2). Stage 2, Route 3—testing weighted L^4–L^3 interpolation, Brezis–Lieb splitting, and Moser iteration—is designed to determine whether such a mechanism exists. If all candidates fail, the obstruction is upgraded from “mechanism-internal” to “model-level”; if one succeeds, Outcome A remains possible. This logical distinction is the concrete expression of the Proved / Conditional / Conjectured separation in the categorisation protocol.

